title: "statement-store: fix unbounded growth of the evicted-hashes map"
doc:
  - audience: Node Dev
    description: |
      The statement store keeps a map of recently-removed statement hashes to
      suppress re-gossip. That map grew unboundedly because:

      1. Naturally-expired statements (whose own expiry timestamp had already
         passed) were inserted unconditionally, even though `submit` already
         rejects them via `AlreadyExpired` without consulting the map.

      2. Purging used `HashMap::retain`, scanning the entire map on every
         maintenance cycle — O(n) with a 48-hour TTL this could accumulate
         ~100M entries (~9 GB RAM).

      This PR fixes both issues:

      - Naturally-expired statements are no longer added to the evicted map
        (or written to `col::EXPIRED`). Re-gossip suppression is still
        guaranteed because `submit` rejects them unconditionally.

      - The map is replaced by `EvictedIndex`, which pairs a `HashSet` for
        O(1) membership tests with a `BTreeMap` keyed by `(purge_at, hash)`
        for O(log n + k) deadline-ordered purges.

      - The purge deadline for a forcibly evicted statement is now
        `min(expiry_timestamp, current_time + purge_after_sec)`, so hashes
        that are close to their natural expiry are drained sooner.

crates:
  - name: sc-statement-store
    bump: patch
